WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.270
AMC's Breaking Bad was a landmark television
drama… and not just because it was so brilliantly

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.280
written, expertly plotted, and beautifully
photographed.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.559
From its charismatic leads to its supporting
cast, the show's talented ensemble turned

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.020
in some of the finest performances ever committed
to the small screen.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.920
And Aaron Paul was quite possibly the show's
Most Valuable Player.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.560
As you probably know, he portrayed Jesse Pinkman,
the hapless accomplice to Bryan Cranston's

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.240
high school chemistry teacher-turned-drug-kingpin
Walter White.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.000
Paul created a character that was incredibly
easy to root for, despite having more than

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.380
his fair share of faults.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.380
He was self-centered, endlessly self-pitying,
and always looking to make a quick buck.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.140
'This is your deal, man.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.340
You want to smoke it, smoke it up at your
house."

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.440
By the end of the series, he'd progressed
from a small-time drug dealer to a reluctant

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.000
murderer.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.520
Of course, Jesse also had a remarkable capacity
for caring, loyalty, and trust qualities which

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.140
unfortunately made him way too easy to manipulate
and exploit.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.800
Paul's portrayal of Pinkman was so remarkable
that it's virtually impossible to imagine

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.380
anyone else stepping into the role.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.960
His nuanced, soulful performance is ultimately
what won fans over and what made Jesse a sympathetic

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:16.460
character.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.100
Interestingly, Paul almost didn't get a chance
to turn Jesse Pinkman into the character we

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.460
all know and love today.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.900
Breaking Bad creator Vince Gilligan originally
intended for Jesse to die at the end of the

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:29.900
first season.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.760
In fact, that death was supposed to raise
the stakes for Walter... and hasten his transformation

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.260
into the ruthless, deadly drug lord known
as Heisenberg.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.460
Even before production on Breaking Bad began,
Gilligan reportedly pitched an idea to studio

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:49.950
executives and they really didn't like it:
Long story short, Jesse would be killed in

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.340
a drug deal gone bad.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.280
Blinded by rage, Walter would subsequently
kidnap the person responsible and hold him

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.980
captive in his basement, torturing him in
unspeakable ways.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.520
According to IndieWire, studio brass managed
to talk Gilligan off that particular ledge,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.060
but he evidently still intended to kill off
Jesse down the line.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.340
As it turns out, Jesse was originally supposed
to meet his maker in the ninth episode of

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:19.129
Breaking Bad's first season… but perhaps
you've noticed that Season One only has seven

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:19.660
episodes.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.920
That's because the 2007 Writers' Guild of
America strike got underway in the middle

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.520
of production, reportedly forcing the powers-that-be
to shorten the first season.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.460
And that was ultimately very good news for
Jesse Pinkman and Aaron Paul.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.060
As the AV Club reports,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.900
"In the intervening time between seasons,
Vince Gilligan changed his mind [about killing

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.440
Jesse]."

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.560
If that's true, it might be the only time
in television history that a character was

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.980
given a stay of execution simply because the
writers refused to do their jobs.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.099
But as Gilligan admits himself, the strike
wasn't the only reason he decided to let Jesse

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:56.600
live.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.750
Speaking at PaleyFest 2010 in Los Angeles,
Gilligan explained his thought process in

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:02.740
a bit more detail.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:08.659
As the principal cast sat next to him including
Paul he revealed the real reason he spared

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.900
Jesse… and it wasn't because the young man
just had so much life left to live.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.860
At one point, Gilligan admits:

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.860
"The original plan was to kill him off but
I have to say, the writer's strike, in a sense,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:20.540
didn't save him."

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.360
Gilligan then tells Paul:

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.640
"I knew by episode 2 we all did, all of us
our wonderful directors and our wonderful

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.860
producers everybody knew just how good you
are, and a pleasure to work with, and it became

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:38.780
pretty clear early on that that would be a
huge, colossal mistake to kill off Jesse."

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.090
Truer words have never been spoken.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.940
Of course, Jesse even managed to outlive Heisenberg
in the end and he eventually came to find

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:50.790
a modicum of peace in El Camino: A Breaking
Bad Movie, the Netflix film that serves as

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.180
a follow-up to the main series.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:57.019
"Question they're going to ask is, What am
I doing here trying to hand over a Hefty bag

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.080
full of cash to a dude who runs a vacuum cleaner
store?"

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:06.400
In the years since Breaking Bad ended, Paul
has continued to have an enviable acting career.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.760
He lent his voice to the acclaimed animated
series BoJack Horseman through the show's

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.140
entire run… and he's set to co-star in Season
3 of Westworld.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.930
No wonder he's in such high demand: He helped
create one of the greatest hour-long television

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.320
dramas ever produced.

